 "verdict": "ADMIT. A FOURTH TRANSPORT PAIR MODE, open and commercially strong, and one Livdar has not built.",
 "why_it_matters": "the air and rail pair axes are already built and wired into the manifest. Ferry is the same shape, it is measured here for the first time, and almost every row names two real places rather than one. It is also the most commercially valuable of the three: the median CPC runs well above the air and rail pairs and the head reaches 3.00 USD.",
 "difficulty": "mostly KD 0 to 15. The exceptions are the famous crossings where an operator owns the SERP (martha's vineyard 40, statue of liberty 53, staten island 45, newfoundland 52).",
 "geography": "the tail is global, not American: Greek islands (athens to milos, santorini to mykonos, paros, crete), the Adriatic (split to hvar, dubrovnik, bari), the Amalfi coast (naples to positano, sorrento to capri, salerno), the Baltic (helsinki to tallinn, copenhagen to oslo), the Channel (dover to calais, england to france), the Irish Sea (holyhead to dublin, ireland to scotland, belfast), Gibraltar (tarifa to tangier, spain to morocco), the Balearics (mallorca to ibiza), the Caribbean (st thomas to st john and to tortola, aruba to curacao, st martin to st barts), Japan and Korea (busan to fukuoka), and Puget Sound and New England.",
 "source": "OpenStreetMap carries route=ferry relations under ODbL 1.0, which is already the licence the POI corpus uses, and the transport graph builder already knows how to turn a route relation into node and edge records. This is the cheapest new pair mode to add.",
 "cardinality": "every scheduled ferry crossing worldwide. OSM ferry routes number in the thousands, and the measured demand reaches 219 pairs at 300 searches or more in English alone.",
 "canonicalisation": "the same rule the air and rail pairs use. Both directions appear as separate keywords with separate parent topics (split to hvar 900 and hvar to split 400; naples to capri and capri to naples; santorini to mykonos and mykonos to athens), but the answer set is identical, so one page per crossing naming both directions.",
